{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak and you shall say, etc. Like the deeds, etc.</b> One must know why He repeated saying <b>Speak and you shall say</b>. Further, what did He innovate with this statement <b>I am HaShem your Elohim</b> — did they not know this until now? And further, what is His intention in the commandment of ״like the deeds, etc.״, if it is not known what the deeds are upon which the commandment comes, and likewise the deeds of the land of Canaan?" "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim) that He made an analogy between the deeds of Egypt and the deeds of Canaan. And according to their words, z\"l, it is difficult: why did He repeat the commandment? And if it is to inform us that the deeds of both wicked nations are the same, what need does the Torah have to inform us of this thing? Further, one must know why He specified the land of Egypt by saying <b>in which you dwelt</b> — is there another land of Egypt? Further, He specified <b>the land of Canaan</b> and said <b>which I am bringing, etc.</b>, which makes it appear that there is another and we would not know any but it. And even if there were another land of Canaan, since it is not known, how would its deeds be known in order to command concerning them?" "<b>And it appears</b> that according to what He juxtaposed to this commandment the commandment of the forbidden relations, and also from what He said at the end of the section (Leviticus 18:24) ״Do not defile yourselves in any of these, etc., for in all these the nations defiled themselves״ — the matter proves and declares that ״the deed of the land of Egypt״ and ״the deed of the land of Canaan״ refers to the deed of forbidden relations. And with this we shall come to explain the verse correctly." "Now it is well known that all the commandments that HaShem commanded to the people of His holiness are commandments that a person is able to stand firm in and incline himself toward the will to perform them — except for the commandment of separation from forbidden relations, which is a thing that a person's soul craves and compels him to do them — unless a person intensifies the distancing of two things from himself, namely: distance from the sensation of sight, and distance from the dimension of thought. And if he does not do both of these, no person is master over his spirit to eliminate it from himself. For as long as he does not distance the sensation of sight in the matter, even if he distances the dimension of thought, he will not master himself to eliminate the compulsion of desire from himself. Go and learn (Kiddushin 81a) from the incident of Rabbi Amram, the prince of the pious, who although he was stripped of the dimension of thought, was defeated by the dimension of sight. Also from the incident of Rabbi Matya ben Harash (Yalkut Shimoni, parashat Vayechi), who chose to blind his eyes because of the compulsion that would come through them toward the evil thing, even though he was stripped of the dimension of thought, as his deeds prove. Furthermore, even if the dimension of sight is removed, if he does not distance the dimension of thought, his thoughts will compel him to seek and become inflamed after the deed. Go and learn from the incident brought in the Talmud (Avodah Zarah 22b) concerning that non-Jew who bought a thigh of meat and dug a hole in it, etc., and violated it, etc. — and this is a consequence of thought compelling the deed even without the dimension of sight. You thus conclude that with the absence of either one of the two, a person's mastery over himself in this matter is nullified — and needless to say when the two things combine, sight and thought, for then the person is feeble against his desire. And needless to say if he sends forth his hand and partakes of a little of this evil — behold, he is delivered into the hands of his desire. From this it follows that a person's stratagems to wage war against this dimension consist in removing two things from himself, and through this there will be established in a person an orientation so that he will not become inflamed after it and the will shall govern the natural desire. And this is the reason for the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Berakhot 34b): in the place where penitents stand, even the perfectly righteous cannot stand — for the perfectly righteous, the orientation of their will conquers desire without need for intensification, unlike penitents." "<b>And with this</b> we shall come to the explanation. Behold, the Master HaShem of Hosts acted wisely in His commanding this commandment, and spoke against the evil inclination, which says: how can a natural human being master his desire, which compels him? This matter is removed from reason — that HaShem should require His observance from everyone equally — except for people who are able to stand firm and have strength in their will to negate desire in this, namely those who have not come to the test of sight and thought. Regarding such people and their like the verse speaks. But regarding one in whom the aforementioned dimension has been breached, according to nature no person is master over his spirit to prevent from himself the compulsion of desire. And to this HaShem intended with the pleasantness of the words of the living Elohim, and said <b>like the deeds of the land of Egypt in which you dwelt</b> — meaning: even though you dwelt in a place whose people are children of harlotry, and the dimension of thought was not withheld from you on account of your dwelling among them all that time, as He said <b>in which you dwelt</b>, and ״dwelling״ means prolonged staying — and this causes the desire to grow strong within you — even so, I command you that you shall strengthen yourselves to conquer the desire so as not to do like their deeds." "It further says <b>and like the practice of the land of Canaan, to which I am bringing you there</b> etc. — meaning: even though you are coming to a place where the she-demon of this aspect of evil has found rest, and consequently the aspect of the evil inclination is aroused, nevertheless I obligate you not to do as their practices." "And because a person might say that there is no restraint on his spirit in this matter, therefore HaShem prefaced His words at the outset and said <b>Speak to the children of Israel and say to them: I am YHWH your Elohim</b> — for it is true that among a human species other than Israel this claim holds, for one will not find in his power the ability to restrain the intensity of his desire from him, which is not so with you, the children of Israel, on account of My being <b>YHWH your Elohim</b> — and this is an elohisk apprehension: by the elohisk power the natural, physical force is vanquished, for the form governs the matter when a man of Israel consents to cleave to YHWH his Elohim — he governs his nature. And this is as they said of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 34): the righteous — their heart is given into their hand, but the wicked — they are given into the hand of their heart. The intention of this is that desire and craving reside in a person's heart, while the will resides in his brain, and these are two levels that the Master has placed in the children of man. When a matter of forbidden relations presents itself to a person, the heart covets it, and there is no completion to this coveting, but the will, which depends on the brain, is able to restrain the desire and not to act upon it even though he covets. You will indeed find among the children of man those who accept a fast: even though desire overpowers them to eat, the will comes and restrains it — and so on along this path. And the righteous always conduct themselves according to this order, and their heart is given into their hand, so that even if a craving arises in their heart, the intellect — which is the soul — will prevail, and if there is a warning from HaShem or from the general principles of abhorrence, it will nullify the will regarding it and restrain the desire even though it exists. But the wicked are given into the hand of their heart: for whatever desire that enters the heart, the will does not restrain it. And perhaps this is because they have no soul, or because they have already blemished it and it is subdued before the spirit that is in the heart. Therefore HaShem said <b>for I am YHWH your Elohim</b> — meaning, so that the aspect of the soul, upon which HaShem bestows the light of His Shekhinah within man, in the sod of (Psalms 78:69) ״אֹהֶל שִׁכֵּן בָּאָדָם״ — and from this aspect there is power in man to vanquish the desire before the will." "<b>And this</b> is what King David, peace be upon him, intended to say in his statement (Psalms 40:9) ״לַעֲשׂוֹת רְצוֹנְךָ אֱלֹהַי חָפַצְתִּי וְתוֹרָתְךָ בְּתוֹךְ מֵעָי״. The intention is that through the abundance of his clinging to the Master of masters, blessed be His name forever, to the point that even the heart is transformed to act as the soul acts — so that desire comes upon it and the craving to do His will, blessed be He; he is not of the class of those who desire things removed from holiness and that need to be restrained through the negation of the will, but rather of his own accord he desires, by His will blessed be He, to hate the things that the heart desires and craves. And this is what he was precise in saying ״חָפַצְתִּי״ — for the felt desire has ceased, and this he said in another place (Psalms 109:22) ״וְלִבִּי חָלַל בְּקִרְבִּי״. And his saying ״וְתוֹרָתְךָ בְּתוֹךְ מֵעָי״ — meaning, through the abundance of craving for the service of holiness, to the point that he feels, in learning the Torah of HaShem, as though he is eating and filling his inward parts with natural sustenance." "<b>Further</b>, it may be that he intended to convey a certain preface that I heard in the name of the holy rabbi, the Ari z'l (Sha'ar Ha-Gilgulim 19), which is that sometimes a person will turn and change from good to evil in his nature and temperament, and he will not know from what cause, and he himself will wonder how his mind has changed. And he said that this is caused because sometimes a person will eat a food that contains a portion from the portions of evil, or it contains an evil soul from the transmigrations, due to their ailments, and when the evil soul or the spark of evil enters within him, it will turn him from good to evil and the aspect of evil will overpower him. From this it follows that one who guards himself from evil foods will have his desire strengthened regarding things that pertain to the soul, and this is his saying ״לַעֲשׂוֹת רְצוֹנְךָ אֱלֹהַי חָפַצְתִּי וְתוֹרָתְךָ בְּתוֹךְ מֵעָי״." "<b>And to give you insight</b> into the meaning of his saying ״וְתוֹרָתְךָ בְּתוֹךְ מֵעָי״, know that everything that grows — whether in things essential for the life of the soul, or in things with special properties in the world, or in things that are human delicacies — the root of all and its coming-into-being is from the Torah, which is the mother of all. And just as in the Torah there are peshat, remez, derash, and sod, so too with the foods of this world. And see what I innovated in my commentary on Song of Songs (Rishon Le-Tzion on Song of Songs 2:4): just as among plants there are things with special and natural properties, so too in the Torah there are commandments with special properties, etc., and this preface is great and precious in value. And I have written these chapter headings to help you understand his saying ״וְתוֹרָתְךָ בְּתוֹךְ מֵעָי״ — for all the food that is eaten: the foundation of its vitality, through which it was brought into being and grew, is from the nourishment of holiness, which is the Torah. And from the time the first man blemished and good and evil became intermingled in all food, you will find a portion of evil. And for this reason HaShem commanded concerning the orlah of trees, and for this reason the chaff and straw and bran that are scattered grow together with the wheat. And HaShem separated us from many things in which the portion of evil holds sway, so as not to defile the chosen soul. And therefore when a person eats something permitted, it is as though he enjoys the supernal light from the luminaries of the light of Torah — except that it became physicalized by virtue of the rectification of the foundations of the earth, which require nourishment according to their preparation and their composition. And this is his saying ״וְתוֹרָתְךָ בְּתוֹךְ מֵעָי״ — thereby his desire and longing to do His will, blessed be He, overpowered, for there is no evil within him that would remove the desire for what is sweet to the soul." "<b>Let us return</b> to the matter we have come to: that the disposition of contemplating a matter of forbidden relations intensifies the desire and imposes it upon the will, and the triumph over the desire will not be achieved except specifically through the utmost removal of the matter from one's awareness and thought, so as not to see the ugliness and not to think about it. But if his eyes gaze upon the matter, behold he is in danger — like the deed of King David, upon him be peace, when the circumstance arose and he saw what he saw (II Samuel 11:2), his heart was stirred, for it was vulnerable, and he did his deed. And even the beginning of thought concerning this matter will cause intensification and the generation of the natural disposition of the desire and its strengthening. Therefore the wisest of all the wise, King Solomon upon him be peace, was moved to say (Proverbs 1:10): ״בְּנִי אִם יְפַתּוּךָ חַטָּאִים״ — meaning, the disposition of sin — ״אַל תֹּאבֶה״ — meaning, do not enter with its enticements into argument and counter-argument, for prolongation of the awareness of the contemplation, even in the manner of negation, will activate the strengthening of the desire and will turn the will toward it. And the clear commandment of HaShem comes upon the matter in the manner of דִּבּוּר (speech) and אֲמִירָה (saying): דִּבּוּר — to declare that the decree of the King is a matter of supreme authority; אֲמִירָה — the language of a gentle response and the language of exaltation, for they exist at this level to say of them <b>אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם</b>, to unify His name, blessed be He, upon them. Also, great is the virtue of one who keeps His covenant, and it is an eminence than which there is none higher. Therefore He said <b>וְאָמַרְתָּ</b>:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:05.108580264Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}